>to plain vanilla Nethack? What would you recommend to a Nethack player 

No, it's quite comparable.

Major like: spellcasting exercises spell knowledge.  I've been playing
wizards a lot in Nethack recently and getting really annoyed by
"forgetting" something like force bolt or magic missile which I'd been
using constantly.

Elves being "good" (lawful or neutral) is a nice change.

Getting the +20 damage Bat Out Of Hell as a Rogue (guaranteed 1st
sacrifice gift) was fun, though I couldn't help feeling it was
distorting: at that point the Rogue switches from a skulking
dagger-thrower to a major combat machine.

Firearms are interesting.  Ostensibly do lots of damage, though grenades
may not damage the fire-resistant.  Pretty heavy, and I've usually not
messed with them.  They, and things like wands of draining, make
soldiers worthy of more respect.

Shopkeepers live longer, since they're (a) more useful, with services
and (b) much tougher.

At higher levels you can actually keep levelling up via experience, vs.
potions of gain level and succubi and wraiths as the only method past
level 12 in Nethack.

I'm not sure about Gehennom.  There's a bit less of it, and fewer maze
levels in exchange for more demon lairs.  Which are often annoying in
their own right... annoyance for annoyance.  Lethe might be interesting
but I guess my version doesn't have the patch.

I could miss the level 3 Identify spell as a wizard, but shopkeeper ID
is cool for everyone else.

Robes are body armor, which is nice for monks and annoying for everyone
else, especially with most body armor, even DSM, hurting spellcasting
somewhat.  In Nethack a Knight can end up being a good spellcaster; it's
not clear that would happen so much in Slash'em.  But I haven't played a
Slash Knight.

>who has never ascended: to keep playing Nethack until she ascends or 
>drop Nethack and start playing Slash 'em?

Slash has been described as "nethack with extra death".  Still, I think
you can go either way.

